# B2 First: ek okuma ve dinleme çalışmaları (özgün metinler)
def mkr(k,id,t,ty,nm,ins,**o):
    d={'k':k,'id':id,'t':t,'ty':ty,'nm':nm,'ti':'Reading and Use of English Part '+k[1],'ins':ins};d.update(o);return d
def mkl(k,id,t,ty,nm,ins,**o):
    d={'k':k,'id':id,'t':t,'ty':ty,'nm':nm,'ti':'Listening Part '+k[1],'ins':ins};d.update(o);return d
CI='Metni oku ve her boşluk için en uygun kelimeyi (A, B, C ya da D) seç.'
R=[
mkr('R5','b2r5c','Uzun metin: Dört günlük çalışma haftası','mc','Okuma 5 · Uzun metin','Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.',title='Is the four-day week the future?',
 p=['For most of the last century, the working week has followed the same pattern: five days at the office, two days off. Recently, however, a growing number of companies have begun to question whether this arrangement still makes sense. In several trials across Europe, employees were asked to work four days a week for the same pay, and the results were surprising.',
  'The most common worry was that productivity would suffer. In practice, the opposite often happened. Workers who knew they had a long weekend ahead became more focused, held shorter meetings and avoided wasting time online. One manager at a software firm admitted that she had expected chaos. "Instead," she said, "people simply stopped doing the things that were never useful in the first place."',
  'Employees also reported that they felt healthier and less stressed. Many used the extra day to see family, exercise or study, and fewer of them called in sick. Companies noticed another benefit: it became much easier to recruit talented staff, because a shorter week was seen as a valuable perk.',
  'Even so, the idea is not suitable for every business. Hospitals, shops and restaurants cannot simply close for an extra day, and some workers ended up squeezing five days of work into four, which left them exhausted. Experts therefore advise that each organisation should design its own version rather than copy a successful example from elsewhere.'],
 q=[{'q':'What does the writer say about trials of the four-day week?','o':['They proved that the idea does not work.','They produced unexpected results.','They were limited to one country.','They were criticised by employees.'],'a':1,'tg':['detail'],'e':'"the results were surprising".'},
  {'q':'Why did productivity often improve?','o':['Workers were paid more.','Meetings were abolished.','People concentrated better and wasted less time.','Managers watched the staff more closely.'],'a':2,'tg':['detail','inference'],'e':'Daha odaklı oldular, toplantılar kısaldı, zaman kaybı azaldı.'},
  {'q':'What did the software manager mean by "people simply stopped doing the things that were never useful"?','o':['Staff refused to follow instructions.','Pointless activities disappeared.','The company lost important clients.','Workers became less friendly.'],'a':1,'tg':['vocab','inference'],'e':'Işe yaramayan işler bırakıldı.'},
  {'q':'What extra advantage did companies notice?','o':['Lower electricity bills','Easier recruitment of good employees','More customers on Fridays','Better office equipment'],'a':1,'tg':['detail'],'e':'"easier to recruit talented staff".'},
  {'q':'What is the writer\'s conclusion?','o':['All businesses should adopt the four-day week immediately.','The idea works only in the technology sector.','Organisations should adapt the idea to their own situation.','The five-day week is still the best system.'],'a':2,'tg':['attitude'],'e':'Her kurum kendi versiyonunu tasarlamalı.'}]),
mkr('R5','b2r5d','Uzun metin: Şehirdeki sesler','mc','Okuma 5 · Uzun metin','Metni oku ve soruları cevapla.',title='The sounds of the city',
 p=['When I moved from a quiet village to a big city, the first thing I noticed was the noise. Traffic roared day and night, neighbours argued through thin walls, and a building site next door started work at seven every morning. For weeks I slept with earplugs and dreamed of the silence I had left behind.',
  'Then something unexpected happened. A friend, a sound artist, invited me on what she called a "listening walk". We spent an hour moving slowly through the streets without speaking, paying attention only to what we could hear. At first, it was just a blur. After a while, though, I began to separate individual sounds: the rhythm of footsteps, a street musician practising scales, the soft hum of a distant train.',
  'My friend explained that most of us have learnt to "switch off" from everyday sounds in order to concentrate, and that this habit, while useful, makes us miss a great deal. Research suggests that people who regularly listen carefully to their surroundings feel calmer, because they no longer treat noise as an enemy.',
  'I would not pretend that I now love the building site. But the city no longer feels hostile. On my morning walk, I find myself listening for the cheerful whistle of the baker, and I have even stopped using my earplugs.'],
 q=[{'q':'How did the writer feel about the city at the beginning?','o':['He found it exciting.','He was disturbed by the noise.','He quickly got used to it.','He enjoyed the sound of the building site.'],'a':1,'tg':['attitude'],'e':'Kulak tıkacıyla uyudu; sessizliği özledi.'},
  {'q':'What was the purpose of the "listening walk"?','o':['To find a quieter street','To record sounds for a project','To pay attention to the sounds around them','To practise a musical instrument'],'a':2,'tg':['detail'],'e':'Yalnızca duyduklarına dikkat ettiler.'},
  {'q':'What does "switch off" mean in paragraph 3?','o':['turn off electrical equipment','stop noticing something','go to sleep early','leave a place quickly'],'a':1,'tg':['vocab'],'e':'Günlük seslere kulağı kapatmak.'},
  {'q':'According to research, why do careful listeners feel calmer?','o':['They avoid noisy places.','They treat noise as an enemy.','They do not see noise as a threat.','They listen to music more often.'],'a':2,'tg':['detail','inference'],'e':'Gürültüyü artık düşman görmüyorlar.'},
  {'q':'How does the writer feel at the end?','o':['He has changed his attitude, but not completely.','He now loves every noise in the city.','He plans to return to the village.','He wants to become a sound artist.'],'a':0,'tg':['attitude'],'e':'"I would not pretend that I now love the building site."'}]),
mkr('R6','b2r6b','Cümle yerleştirme: Şehirde orman','gap','Okuma 6 · Cümle yerleştirme','Metinden altı cümle çıkarıldı. Her boşluk için uygun cümleyi (A–G) seç. Bir cümle fazladır.',title='A forest in the city',
 p='In the middle of a busy industrial district, a small forest has appeared on a piece of land that was once a car park. (1) ______ It was planted by volunteers using a method that creates dense woodland in a very small space.\nThe idea came from a Japanese botanist, who noticed that natural forests grow quickly when many local species are planted close together. (2) ______ Within three years, the trees were taller than the volunteers who had planted them.\nThe benefits are already visible. The temperature on the site is several degrees lower than in the surrounding streets. (3) ______ Birds, insects and even a family of hedgehogs have moved in.\nResidents have welcomed the project. Children from the local school visit every week to measure the trees and to record the animals they find. (4) ______ "I used to walk past this place with my eyes on my phone," says one neighbour. "Now I slow down and listen."\nOf course, there are limits to what a small wood can achieve. (5) ______ Even so, the organisers hope that other cities will follow their example. (6) ______',
 opts=[['A','It cannot replace the large forests that have been lost.'],['B','They have already received requests for advice from five countries.'],['C','The site is no larger than a tennis court.'],['D','Wildlife has also benefited.'],['E','The trees compete for light, so they grow upwards very fast.'],['F','Many of the volunteers had never planted a tree before.'],['G','Several shops in the district have closed.']],
 a=['C','E','D','F','A','B'],tg=[['coherence'],['coherence'],['coherence'],['reference','coherence'],['reference'],['coherence']],
 e=['Orman çok küçük: tenis kortundan büyük değil (C).','Ağaçlar ışık için yarışır, bu yüzden hızlı büyür; ardından "Within three years" (E).','Sıcaklık düştü; vahşi yaşam da yararlandı (D).','Çocuklar ve gönüllüler; "many of the volunteers" bağlamı (F).','"Of course, there are limits": küçük orman büyük ormanların yerini tutmaz (A).','Diğer şehirlerin örnek alması; beş ülkeden tavsiye talebi (B).']),
mkr('R1','b2r1d','Çoktan seçmeli boşluk: Kent arıcılığı','cloze','Okuma 1 · Çoktan seçmeli boşluk',CI,title='Beekeeping on city roofs',
 p='Keeping bees on the roof of an office block may sound (1) ______, but urban beekeeping is becoming more and more common. In fact, city bees often (2) ______ better than their country cousins, because gardens, parks and balconies provide a wide variety of flowers throughout the year.\nThe first (3) ______ to remember is that bees are not aggressive if they are treated with respect. Beekeepers wear protective clothing and use smoke to keep the insects calm when they open the hive. Most companies that (4) ______ hives report that their employees quickly become fond of them.\nHoney from a city roof has its own special taste, (5) ______ on the plants that grow nearby. Some producers even sell it under the name of the district it comes from. However, experts (6) ______ out that bees alone cannot save the environment. Cities also need to protect wild insects, (7) ______ they too are essential for pollination. Nevertheless, as long as people are willing to learn, the number of rooftop hives is (8) ______ to keep growing.',
 o=[(['unusual','rare','single','particular'],0,['collocation']),(['do','make','work','get'],0,['collocation']),(['thing','matter','point','item'],0,['collocation']),(['install','establish','instal','settle'],0,['collocation']),(['depending','relying','based','resting'],0,['prepositions-in-on-at']),(['point','tell','show','explain'],0,['collocation']),(['since','whereas','unless','although'],0,['linkers-contrast-purpose']),(['likely','sure','possible','probable'],0,['collocation'])],
 e=['sound unusual: alışılmadık gelmek.','do well: iyi gelişmek (bees do better).','The first thing to remember: akılda tutulacak ilk şey.','install hives: kovan kurmak.','depending on: -e bağlı olarak (taste depending on the plants).','point out: belirtmek, işaret etmek.','since: çünkü (yabani böcekler de gerekli).','be likely to: -mesi muhtemel.']),
mkr('R3','b2r3b','Açık boşluk: Erteleme alışkanlığı','open','Okuma 3 · Açık boşluk','Metni oku ve her boşluğa TEK kelime yaz.',title='Why do we put things off?',one=True,
 p='Almost everybody has, at some time, left an important task until the last moment. Psychologists call this habit procrastination, and it is far (1) ______ common than most people admit. Contrary to popular belief, it is not simply a sign of laziness. In fact, many people who put things off are hard-working individuals who worry (2) ______ doing the job badly.\nResearchers have found that we tend to prefer a pleasant activity now (3) ______ a better reward later. Checking your phone feels good immediately, (4) ______ finishing a report does not. The result is that unpleasant tasks keep moving to tomorrow, and then to the day after.\nSo how can we break the habit? One simple trick is to divide a big task into small steps. If you tell yourself that you will only work for ten minutes, you are (5) ______ likely to start. Another useful tip is to remove distractions, such as turning (6) ______ notifications. Finally, it helps to forgive yourself. People who criticise themselves harshly (7) ______ end up delaying even more, (8) ______ feeling guilty makes the task seem more frightening.',
 a=[['more'],['about'],['than','to','over'],['but','while','whereas','yet'],['more'],['off'],['often','usually','tend','sometimes'],['because','since','as']],
 tg=[['comparison-advanced'],['prepositions-in-on-at'],['comparison-advanced'],['linkers-contrast-purpose'],['comparison-advanced'],['prepositions-in-on-at'],['present-simple'],['linkers-contrast-purpose']],
 e=['far more common: çok daha yaygın.','worry about: endişelenmek.','prefer ... to ...: ... yerine ...yi tercih etmek.','but / whereas: karşıtlık.','more likely: daha olası.','turn off: kapatmak.','often end up: çoğu zaman sonunda ... olur.','because: çünkü.']),
]
